body:
I've put a beta out with some basic functionality: https://github.com/jonhadfield/soba/releases/tag/1.2.8-beta
In trying to keep it as simple as possible, I'll send a webhook on completion if env var `SOBA_WEBHOOK_URL` is provided (allowing for custom destination), and allow specifying a format of either `short` or `long` (the default, if not set to short) by setting `SOBA_WEBHOOK_FORMAT`.

This is what the short format looks like:
```
{
  "app": "soba",
  "type": "backups.complete",
  "stats": {
    "succeeded": 14,
    "failed": 0
  },
  "timestamp": "2024-02-11T12:35:08Z",
  "data": {
    "started_at": "2024-02-11T12:35:01Z",
    "finished_at": "2024-02-11T12:35:08Z"
  }
}
```

with the default (long) format as:
```
{
  "app": "soba",
  "type": "backups.complete",
  "stats": {
    "succeeded": 5,
    "failed": 0
  },
  "timestamp": "2024-02-11T12:34:39Z",
  "data": {
    "started_at": "2024-02-11T12:34:32Z",
    "finished_at": "2024-02-11T12:34:39Z",
    "results": [
      {
        "provider": "GitHub",
        "results": [
          {
            "repo": "go-soba/repo1",
            "status": "ok"
          },
          {
            "repo": "go-soba/repo0",
            "status": "ok"
          },
          {
            "repo": "Nudelmesse/private2",
            "status": "ok"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "provider": "GitLab",
        "results": [
          {
            "repo": "biscuits2/bourbon",
            "status": "ok"
          },
          {
            "repo": "soba-test/my-soba-group-project",
            "status": "ok"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

With regards to heartbeat, I assume anyone using a webhook would perform some kind of parsing, so I'm thinking a check of `stats` for a positive `succeeded` number and a 0 `failed` number should suffice.  
I do see the value of separation for heartbeat, or maybe even using long format for error scenarios only; i'm just trying to minimise complexity and bombarding the average user with too many options. If that makes sense?  

Let me know what you think.
